{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"DESIGN (option 2, for operator approval) — restructure the enrich drain so LLM calls run OUTSIDE the drain's advisory lock. Design-only investigation (2026-08-22, Copilot CLI session); no code changed, no Gerrit pushes. Experiments ran in a scratch worktree (design-6148-exp, retired) against throwaway stores.\n\n== 1. MECHANICS AS VALIDATED (path:line, working tree @ origin/main) ==\n\nTwo different locks are in play; the 437s/131s holds were the DRAIN lock, not the store write lock:\n\n- STORE WRITE LOCK: `_store/lock.py` (`write_lock`/`acquire`, dual-window fcntl+mkdir, `.ticket-write.lock[.d]` inside the tracker). Protects invariant I5 (single locked append+commit path — docs/concurrency.md §I5). Held per-event for stage+commit only (event_append.py:166, 284). The drain NEVER holds it across an LLM call: each queue write funnels through `queue._append` (queue.py:82) -> `_seam.append_event` -> the locked append, acquired and released per event. queue.py:23-28 documents this explicitly (\"a claim CANNOT hold lock.acquire across its own append... The epic's design is therefore optimistic\").\n- DRAIN ADVISORY LOCK: `enrich-drain.lock` sidecar via `stamped_lock.stamped_file_lock` (enrich_drain.py:108-116; stamped_lock.py:12-13: \"These are NOT store write locks: they only stop two PROCESSES doing redundant work\"). The correctness boundary is the per-ticket optimistic CLAIM_ENRICH + lease (queue.py:454 `claim`, stamped_lock.py accepted-race comment) — the drain lock protects NO store invariant, only LLM spend.\n\nThe defect: `drain()` acquires the drain lock (enrich_drain.py:272) and holds it in a `finally` (:320) across the WHOLE batch — self-heal re-enqueues (:290-293), the pending scan (:295), and up to `overlap_drain_batch=5` (llm/config.py:150) SEQUENTIAL `enrich()` LLM calls (:309) plus 3 store appends+pushes per item (claim :300, digest emit :310, mark_done :311). Hence the observed 437s/131s holds. Meanwhile `maybe_drain` fires on BOTH write seams (event_append.py:357 via `_maybe_enrich_drain` :394-402, after the locked commit+push; push.py:591-598) and, while anything is soaked, spawns a detached child per write (enrich_drain.py:393-396) — every one of which hits the held lock and exits `{\"skipped\": \"lock-held\"}` (:275-281). So the system is structurally single-flight: throughput ceiling = 5 tickets per lock-hold (minutes), against enqueues from every certification (plan_review/attest.py:211-219, soak 60min, config.py:141) plus self-heal re-enqueues of every present-stale digest (:290-293) — and any post-enrichment ticket edit makes its digest present-stale (digest_sidecar.py:160-190, fail-closed), so ordinary activity feeds the backlog. The per-write ~3s tax is the gate probe `pending_enrichment` (enrich_drain.py:383 -> queue.py:414): with a standing past-soak backlog the marker cannot prove quiet, so every probe re-reduces all ~1,046 live entries (queue.py:426-440, O(backlog), 3 listdirs/entry).\n\nConfig doors (unchanged by this design): REBAR_LLM_OVERLAP_DRAIN off|async|always (config.py:149,437-439,723), REBAR_LLM_OVERLAP_DRAIN_BATCH (:150), gate budget (:151), overlap_soak_min=60/overlap_lease_ttl_min=15 (:141-142).\n\nDedicated-clone drain, validated for completeness: the store is the `tickets` orphan branch worktree (docs/concurrency.md §Storage shape). A drain in its OWN clone/worktree of that branch would append events under its own checkout's write lock (lock files are per-checkout), commit, and push to origin/tickets; convergence is the existing merge-as-union (I2 globally-unique event filenames make add-add merges conflict-free; §Outbound push). It is feasible and I6-compliant (leases, no new cross-client lock), but it fixes a DIFFERENT problem (foreground store-lock/push contention from the drain's per-item appends), costs a second store checkout + freshness lag (must fetch before reads), and does not by itself fix single-flight or the lock-across-LLM hold.\n\n== 2. EXPERIMENT RESULTS (scratch stores, stubbed runner via drain()'s existing `runner=` seam) ==\n\nE1 baseline (8 tickets, 5 enqueued soak-0, stub LLM sleeping 3s): drain lock held 19.8s for 5 items (15s LLM + ~4.8s queue-write overhead); 27/27 rival drain attempts during the hold returned lock-held (zero parallelism); foreground `rebar comment` writes were NOT blocked (median 0.20s = normal commit cost) — confirming the drain lock starves the DRAIN pipeline, not writers directly; writers pay via the gate probe and the drain's own append/push contention.\n\nE1b gate-probe scaling: with live-but-SOAKING entries the marker fast path is O(1) (~0.1ms at 50/200/400 entries — `next_eligible` in the future proves quiet). With the entries PAST soak (the production shape) the scoped walk engages: ~10ms @50, ~150-195ms @200, ~160-390ms @400 pending, uncontended local SSD — linear in backlog, consistent with the measured 2.8-3.2s at ~1,046 under 20-agent contention. The backlog, not marker churn, prices the probe.\n\nE2 prototype (`drain_v2`: claim-under-drain-lock -> LLM outside -> revalidate+writeback): lock hold fell 19.8s -> 1.5-2.9s (the claim window only, ~13x); two CONCURRENT drains processed 10 tickets with `llm_calls_total == 10` — claim arbitration gave exactly one LLM call per ticket, zero redundant spend, both drains productive.\n\nE3 failure modes: (stale) claim, edit ticket, write back old-state digest -> freshness reads \"present-stale\" (fail-closed) and `_stale_digest_ids` re-enqueues — today's system tolerates stale write-backs already, at the cost of a re-enqueue loop; with revalidation the write-back is skipped and the entry re-enqueued pending (measured: stale_skipped=1, requeued_pending=true). (crash) claim with 1.2s lease, die before writeback -> entry claimed during lease, pending after expiry, next drain processes it (processed=1). (race) covered by E2.\n\n== 3. OSS SURVEY — how others run expensive work against a lock-protected queue ==\n\n- Sidekiq: atomic BRPOP reserve; job held in worker memory during processing, no Redis lock held (lib/sidekiq/fetch.rb:36-40); Sidekiq Pro super_fetch adds RPOPLPUSH to a per-process backup list + orphan sweep (wiki \"Reliability\").\n- Celery `task_acks_late`: broker ACK deferred until after execution; crash -> broker redelivery / visibility-timeout expiry (docs.celeryq.dev configuration.html#task-acks-late).\n- Postgres SKIP LOCKED queues — the canonical shape: claim rows in a SHORT transaction (`SELECT ... FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED`), COMMIT (releasing the lock), execute with NO lock held, finalize in a second short transaction, with lease/heartbeat crash recovery. solid_queue (`ReadyExecution.claim` -> `claimed_executions` insert; process heartbeat + supervisor reaping), Oban (`fetch_jobs` CTE FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED -> state=executing; Lifeline plugin rescues stale executing jobs), pg-boss (fetch -> active + `expireInSeconds`; complete() finalizes), good_job (pg advisory lock per job).\n- git itself: index.lock held only for the index mutation; fetch/push write objects to a quarantine dir with no index lock; `gc --auto` detaches and gc.pid only prevents CONCURRENT gc (git-scm.com/docs/git-gc, git-receive-pack §Quarantine) — exactly the pattern enrich_drain's module docstring already cites.\n- Bazel Remote Worker API: lease PENDING->ACTIVE->COMPLETED with session expire_time; a missed heartbeat reassigns the lease (googleapis remoteworkers/v1test2/bots.proto).\n\nBEST MAP: the SKIP LOCKED / pg-boss shape (reserve-short, process-unlocked, finalize-short, lease recovery). rebar's queue ALREADY IS this pattern at the per-ticket layer — CLAIM_ENRICH+lease is a visibility timeout, DONE_ENRICH is the ack, digest emit is idempotent (overwrite-by-content-hash). The only deviation from the pattern is the process-level drain lock held across the PROCESS phase. The fix is therefore a restructure of drain() alone, not new queue machinery.\n\n== 4. RECOMMENDED DESIGN ==\n\nRestructure `drain()` into three phases (behavior-preserving extraction along the existing seams, which also serves the complexity ratchet):\n\n- PHASE A — COLLECT (under the drain advisory lock; seconds): self-heal re-enqueues; `pending_enrichment` scan; `claim()` up to the batch cap; snapshot each claimed ticket's state + `digest_sidecar.content_hash`. Release the lock. The drain lock keeps exactly its legitimate role: preventing N concurrent children from redundant SCANNING and stampede-claiming.\n- PHASE B — ENRICH (NO lock held): run `enrich()` per claimed item. A hung provider call now blocks only this process's batch, not every other drainer.\n- PHASE C — FINALIZE (no drain lock; store write lock per append as today, unchanged): re-read the ticket; if content_hash changed since collect -> skip emit, re-enqueue (staleness handled BEFORE the write instead of by the self-heal loop after it); else `ds.emit` + `mark_done`.\n\nLock discipline: store write lock — unchanged, never held across LLM (already true). Drain lock — shrinks from O(batch x LLM latency) to O(scan+claims) (measured 1.5-2.9s). Correctness boundary — unchanged: optimistic claim + lease; crash mid-B/C is healed by lease expiry (E3), a racing drain after lease expiry costs at worst one redundant idempotent digest.\n\nThroughput: with the hold shrunk, the ALREADY-EXISTING per-write spawned children (enrich_drain.py:393-396) become productive concurrent drainers instead of lock-held skips — parallelism with no new spawner, scheduler, or CI trigger. Bound it with a cheap concurrency guard in Phase A (e.g., skip when `status()` shows >= N live claims, N configurable, default small — see OQ1).\n\nStaleness: three independent layers — C's revalidation (new, prevents the wasted write), the fail-closed freshness reader (existing), the stale-digest self-heal (existing, becomes the backstop it was meant to be instead of a churn source).\n\nMigration path: (1) land the drain() restructure + tests (single change, no event-schema or queue change, no store migration — the queue events and their semantics are untouched, so old and new drains interoperate: an old drain just still holds the lock long while new ones wait/skip); (2) separately decide the backlog disposition (OQ2) and the existence-only gate probe (OQ4); (3) optionally raise/adapt the batch cap once holds are short (OQ3).\n\nBlast radius: `src/rebar/llm/enrich_drain.py` (drain() -> 3 helpers) and `tests/unit/test_enrich_drain.py`; possibly a small `queue.py` helper for the revalidation read; docs/llm-framework.md note. NOT touched: `_store/*` (lock.py, event_append.py, push.py seams), queue event types/reducer, MCP surface, config keys. Compatibility: REBAR_LLM_OVERLAP_DRAIN off|async|always semantics preserved; `overlap_drain_batch` keeps its meaning (claim-window size); trigger remains operation-linked on the write path — no CI-provider dependency, so project.portability holds.\n\nDedicated-clone drain: NOT recommended now. It addresses foreground store-lock/push contention from the drain's per-item appends, which this restructure does not — but that contention is 3 short appends per item, and the restructure removes the dominant costs (single-flight + lock-across-LLM). Keep it as a measured follow-on if drain appends still hurt after this lands.\n\n== 5. OPEN QUESTIONS FOR THE OPERATOR ==\n\nOQ1. Concurrent-drain cap: how many parallel drain processes may spend LLM $ at once (default proposal: 2-3, config-keyed)?\nOQ2. Backlog disposition for the standing ~1,046: bulk-drain (≈1,046 trivial-class calls of $ spend), age-out/tombstone entries for closed tickets or entries older than X, or just let the restructured drain eat it?\nOQ3. Batch cap: keep 5 (per-run claim window) or size to the measured enqueue rate once holds are short?\nOQ4. Is the existence-only gate probe (early-exit bool for maybe_drain, kills the per-write O(backlog) tax) in this ticket's scope or a sibling ticket? It is the direct fix for THIS ticket's AC2; the drain restructure fixes the cause of the backlog that prices it.\nOQ5. Stale-at-writeback re-enqueue: soak 0 (immediate retry, my prototype) or full soak (debounce a hot-edited ticket)?\nOQ6. Should the drain's enrich() calls get a tighter explicit per-attempt timeout than the general cfg.timeout_s, given the observed 437s no-progress network wait?\n"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787448819813491001,"uuid":"a9713462-e915-4589-a904-ab03398fd87d"}